1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,130
A lot of small businesses still
handle documents the hard way.

2
00:00:04,150 --> 00:00:06,410
A customer emails a PDF,

3
00:00:06,430 --> 00:00:09,350
someone opens it, finds
the important fields,

4
00:00:09,370 --> 00:00:11,900
types them into a spreadsheet or system,

5
00:00:11,920 --> 00:00:14,990
renames the file and saves
it in the right folder.

6
00:00:15,010 --> 00:00:16,880
It works, but it is slow,

7
00:00:16,900 --> 00:00:18,530
and it is where mistakes happen.

8
00:00:18,550 --> 00:00:21,440
The problem is that
documents are not consistent.

9
00:00:21,460 --> 00:00:23,810
One form puts the address at the top,

10
00:00:23,830 --> 00:00:25,280
another puts it at the bottom.

11
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:27,470
Some invoices have the same vendor name

12
00:00:27,490 --> 00:00:29,540
with slightly different formats.

13
00:00:29,560 --> 00:00:31,640
Important information is missing

14
00:00:31,660 --> 00:00:32,810
or hard to read,

15
00:00:32,830 --> 00:00:35,270
so the person doing intake ends up doing

16
00:00:35,290 --> 00:00:38,210
manual detective work for every file.

17
00:00:38,230 --> 00:00:39,800
That is where costs show up.

18
00:00:39,820 --> 00:00:43,250
Data entry errors lead
to follow-ups and rework,

19
00:00:43,270 --> 00:00:45,620
missing fields delay processing,

20
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:47,780
duplicate records get created,

21
00:00:47,800 --> 00:00:51,500
and as volume increases,
the backlog grows quickly.

22
00:00:51,520 --> 00:00:53,270
Here is a cleaner approach.

23
00:00:53,290 --> 00:00:55,760
When an email arrives with an attachment,

24
00:00:55,780 --> 00:00:58,070
the workflow saves the file,

25
00:00:58,090 --> 00:00:59,930
extracts the key fields,

26
00:00:59,950 --> 00:01:02,690
validates them against simple rules,

27
00:01:02,710 --> 00:01:05,600
and writes the results to
a database or spreadsheet.

28
00:01:05,620 --> 00:01:07,940
If something is missing or uncertain,

29
00:01:07,960 --> 00:01:10,400
it routes the document to a review queue

30
00:01:10,420 --> 00:01:11,630
instead of guessing.

31
00:01:11,650 --> 00:01:13,490
It's designed to be safe.

32
00:01:13,510 --> 00:01:16,940
It handles duplicates by
checking for an existing record,

33
00:01:16,960 --> 00:01:18,410
it keeps an audit log

34
00:01:18,430 --> 00:01:20,420
of what was extracted and when,

35
00:01:20,440 --> 00:01:23,330
and it alerts the owner
if the extraction fails.

36
00:01:23,350 --> 00:01:26,540
It also keeps the original
file linked to the record,

37
00:01:26,560 --> 00:01:30,140
so the team can verify anything instantly.

38
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:32,240
Here is an illustrative estimate.

39
00:01:32,260 --> 00:01:36,500
If manual document intake takes
about 5 minutes per document

40
00:01:36,520 --> 00:01:39,620
and this reduces it to
about 1 minute of review,

41
00:01:39,640 --> 00:01:42,020
that saves about 4 minutes per document.

42
00:01:42,040 --> 00:01:44,090
At 300 documents per month,

43
00:01:44,110 --> 00:01:46,280
that's about 1,200 minutes saved,

44
00:01:46,300 --> 00:01:49,640
or roughly 20 hours saved per month.

45
00:01:49,660 --> 00:01:51,290
This is based on assumptions

46
00:01:51,310 --> 00:01:53,300
and is not a guaranteed result.

47
00:01:53,320 --> 00:01:55,790
Independent portfolio prototype.

48
00:01:55,810 --> 00:01:56,960
Fictional data.

49
00:01:56,980 --> 00:01:58,190
Results vary.

50
00:01:58,210 --> 00:02:00,080
The goal is faster intake,

51
00:02:00,100 --> 00:02:02,090
fewer data entry mistakes,

52
00:02:02,110 --> 00:02:03,470
and a clear review process

53
00:02:03,490 --> 00:02:06,140
when something is unclear.
